\transclude{00HM}   % Simple Euclidean algorithm
\transclude{00HN}   % Example (60,18)

\p{This seems a bit tedious—and indeed, we can do slightly better.  Notice that we end up repeatedly subtracting #{b} from #{a}: ##{\gcd(a,b) = \gcd(a-b,b) = \gcd(a-2b,b) = \gcd(a-3b,b) = \dots} And we keep doing this until #{a} is finally smaller than #{b}. But that's [exactly what #{\mod} does](00DE)!  This leads to the following observation:}

\transclude{00F8}   % GCD/mod

\p{Notice that by the division algorithm, #{a \mod b} is guaranteed to be strictly smaller than #{b}. So if we replace #{\gcd(a,b)} with #{\gcd(a \mod b, b)}, the very next thing we would do is to switch the two arguments; hence, we might as well combine these two steps into one.  This finally leads to the most elegant, modern formulation of the algorithm.}

\transclude{00F9}   % Euclidean Alg.
